Date: Fri, 07 Jan 94 22:54:20 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: BOT: How do trees drink?

I wondered:

> How do trees transport water from their roots to their leaves? 

With a bit of help from my friends, here's a possible answer:  Trees are
able to lift water beyond 33 feet (= 1 atmosphere) because of their
strong hydrogen bonds.  Pure water has a high tensile strength (=> high
latent heat of evaporation).  Provided the sap is free from particles
for bubbles to nucleate around (including the sides of the xylem tube)
then high negative pressures (of perhaps thousands of atmospheres,
according to my maths) can be withstood before the sap starts boiling.

Thus trees may grow as tall (almost) as they like and still drink.

Mike Price                         price@price.demon.co.uk

PS Perhaps the xylem tubes are narrow for structural support and to
prevent implosion.

