X-Men/Women,

Time for another legal lesson.  Dani Eder wrote:

    . . . A child is just another product of the body, which
    the mother has invested much effort in creating, and
    which the parents then expend much further effort in
    raising - i.e. converting from a useless raw material
    - a baby - into a useful end product - an adult. . . .

    In this view, you own your own life, but your parents
    stick you with a bill for child-rearing at the time you
    are old enough to contract. . . .

At common law, there is a concept called the "officious
intermeddler" (OI, hereafter).  An example would be the case
where a neighbor decides your lawn needs mowing.  While you are
at work, he comes over and mows your lawn without your permission
or knowledge.  When you come home, he hands you a bill for mowing
your lawn.  Do you have to pay?

The common law says the neighbor is an OI.  He did what he did
because he wanted to.  You own him nothing.  And if he cut down
any of your prize roses in the process, you can sue him for
damages.  (Even if you loved what he did, he is still guilty
of trespass, by the way.)

If you believe in self-ownership, you owe your parents nothing.
They gave you birth and reared you for their own reasons.  You
don't owe them any more than you owe the IRS to pay the National
Debt.

But what if your parents (or the government) stick you with a
bill when you are "old enough to contract"?  Well, it depends,
but in most cases, you still owe nothing.  I'll leave the reasons
why, as an exercise for the reader, and for future posts.


