Date: Fri, 7 Jan 1994 22:44:25 -0500
From: ddf2@postoffice.mail.cornell.edu (David Friedman)
Subject: Libel (Was: No More Prisons)

Freeman Craig Presson writes:

>As for libel and slander complaints being "nonsensical", just remember
>that it requires _intent_, _credibility_, and _untruth_ to carry a
>libel or slander complaint, and that slander was actionable under
>common law.
>
>Something expressed clearly as an opinion is not libelous. I can say 
>I don't like Chrysler minivans and don't trust Chrysler to make a
>reliable car, blah blah, all day without libeling Chrysler. But if I
>tell you "I have it on good authority that Chrysler minvan engines
>have been seizing up as soon as the warranty runs out and the company
>has paid millions in bribes to hush it up." with no basis in fact
>but with obvious intent to damage Chrysler Corp., I claim that should
>be actionable. I am responsible for my statements, after all.

And, again:

"Under current law, to prove libel you have to show _falsehood_ and
_credibility_. Truth is a perfect defense against a libel suit; less
perfect, but still effective, is a showing that no reasonable person
would have believed what was said. The essence of the suit is that a
knowing falsehood was written or spoken with intent to harm a person's
reputation and therefore livelihood. Your arguments for "let the
reader beware" are beguiling but ignore the fraudulent nature of a
true act of libel."

I believe this is mistaken in at least two respects. To begin with, suppose
I am a newspaper and publish the (true) statement that "Jones says Smith is
a convicted murderer." Further suppose that although Jones said it, Smith
is not in fact a convicted murderer. Smith can sue me for publishing a
libel and win.

Suppose I try to protect myself by publishing "Jones says Smith is a
convicted murderer, but this newspaper believes he is not." Now I am not
only guilty of publishing a libel, I am guilty of publishing a libel which
I believed was false--which means that I lose even if Smith is a public
figure and thus (under New York Times v Sullivan) can only sue for
deliberate or reckless libel.

Second, I do not believe that the falsehood must be either knowing or
spoken with malicious intent. That, roughly, is the difference between the
requirement for libeling a public figure and the requirement for libeling
an ordinary person. If Jones says Smith is a convicted murderer because
Jones believes it is true, that is still libel (or slander, if only stated
and not published).

I am not an expert in defamation law, but I did ask someone who was about
part of this when it came up in a course I was teaching, so I am pretty
sure it is right.

